WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.325 --> 00:00:03.625
Merge and rebase both bring a feature branch up to date with

2
00:00:03.475 --> 00:00:04.325
main.

3
00:00:04.300 --> 00:00:06.800
They differ in the history they leave behind.

4
00:00:07.775 --> 00:00:11.775
Feature branched off at commit B. Since then, main gained commit C,

5
00:00:11.750 --> 00:00:15.150
and feature gained D and E. The branches have diverged.

6
00:00:16.125 --> 00:00:20.338
git merge feature creates a new merge commit, M, with two parents: C and

7
00:00:20.188 --> 00:00:20.900
E.

8
00:00:21.625 --> 00:00:23.125
Nothing is rewritten.

9
00:00:23.150 --> 00:00:27.225
D and E keep their original hashes, so the graph records exactly

10
00:00:27.075 --> 00:00:28.900
what happened, in parallel.

11
00:00:29.875 --> 00:00:34.237
git rebase main, run on feature, lifts D and E off and replays them

12
00:00:34.087 --> 00:00:35.325
on top of C.

13
00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.575
The results, D prime and E prime, are new commits with new

14
00:00:39.425 --> 00:00:40.500
hashes.

15
00:00:40.475 --> 00:00:43.950
The history becomes one straight line, as if you started the work

16
00:00:43.800 --> 00:00:44.575
today.

17
00:00:45.575 --> 00:00:49.300
Merge keeps the true history and resolves conflicts once.

18
00:00:49.312 --> 00:00:53.475
Rebase gives a clean line, but you may resolve conflicts commit by

19
00:00:53.325 --> 00:00:54.150
commit.

20
00:00:55.125 --> 00:00:57.800
Rebase freely on work only you have.

21
00:00:57.850 --> 00:01:02.125
Never rebase commits others already pulled: their copies still point at the

22
00:01:01.975 --> 00:01:04.550
old hashes, and the histories split.
